#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558895 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558900 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{128}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558904 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=5 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $5 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(5 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/39/39d20dd045c97cae079f3839b4db071bd6b3b9ff133299ed4ae4d9421a55fee8.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}5&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(7)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{7}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{1}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}[v]_{u^{2}}^{1}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\int_{-1}^{1}(1 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + u]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}(((1)+\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((-1)+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558917 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0$ and the line $x + y=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=8 x - 2 y,\\,v=x + y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $8 x - 2 y=u$ and $x + y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(8 x - 2 y)^{2}-(x + y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x + y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=9.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 3,9).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18c4c61a55dd2a361b82a208eb9a0d26f3fcfa213fe83b05f23644c9f79becaa.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}8&-2\\\\1&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(10)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{1}{10}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{9}dv]du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}[v]_{u^{2}}^{9}du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}\\int_{-3}^{3}(9 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 9 u]_{-3}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(((27)+(-9))-((-27)+(9))) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{10}(36) \\\\ &=\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558926 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4$ and the line $y=8.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 4 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 4 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 4 y)^{2}-(y)=4\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=4\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2} - 4.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=8.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} - 4 \\\\ v &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2 \\sqrt{3},8).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,- 2 \\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le 2 \\sqrt{3},\\,u^{2} - 4\\le v\\le 8\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/23/2369c3299b61f996c6203e1e45a7a62c74cf9e8f4691c2d0941bdeec0a5ae53c.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-4\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[\\int_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}[v]_{u^{2} - 4}^{8}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(8)-(u^{2} - 4)du \\\\ &=1\\int_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}}(12 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 12 u]_{- 2 \\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{3}} \\\\ &=1(((24 \\sqrt{3})+(- 8 \\sqrt{3}))-((- 24 \\sqrt{3})+(8 \\sqrt{3}))) \\\\ &=1(32 \\sqrt{3}) \\\\ &=32 \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558932 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e863fbe1fbcc931a1acaf67fe92990d4bd0551f8c5a1a89a01c8eeba367d668.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):2\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{2}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{2}^{5}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(4\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((2)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(16\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 609 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558943 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}-2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}- 2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 175 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558949 :question/problem "![](images/37/37c3ffbceecd99f2c618c1401e392b27620a0e34ce284683446eae4217c5e973.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{4}3 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}3 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{1}^{4}\\\\ & =6 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((1)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{765 \\pi}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558956 :question/problem "![](images/42/424cfad485da7dd6cac42c9784df348a2a09d2bb4da651926cae32aa6e06cded.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):1\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2\\left(r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\right)\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}2 r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{1}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{1}^{5}\\\\ & =4 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((1)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\pi\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =624 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558965 :question/problem "![](images/b7/b73bdd66e1e9ac758c98d854bfe93b6fcf82ff8d85322f607e50b75a5797770f.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{5}\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =272 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558967 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x^{2} y + y^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=- x^{3} - x y^{2}.$ So,\n\nFor $\\partial P/\\partial y$ we hold $x$ fixed, and for $\\partial Q/\\partial x$ we hold $y$ fixed. The component derivatives, including zero contributions, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y + y^{3}\\right)=\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)=x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{3} - x y^{2}\\right)=\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D\\left(- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}-(x^{2} + 3 y^{2})\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-1\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 2 \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 350 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558981 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,3 u + 5 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 u + 5 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 3,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{9+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{14}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558989 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle 2 u^{2},\\,u - v^{2},\\,v^{2}\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 4 u,\\,1,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(2 u^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u - v^{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(v^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 0,\\,- 2 v,\\,2 v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 u & 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 2 v,\\,- 8 u v,\\,- 8 u v\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(2 v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2+\\left(- 8 u v\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}+64 u^{2} v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{128 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{2}} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2} \\left(32 u^{2} + 1\\right)} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2}}\\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1} \\\\  &=2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{v^2}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D 2 v \\sqrt{32 u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558993 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\quad D=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - v,\\,5 u + 7 v + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,5\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(u + v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(3 - v\\right),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\left(5 u + 7 v + 1\\right)\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,7\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v &=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  &=\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\rangle \\\\  &=\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\| &=\\left\\|\\langle 5,\\,-2,\\,-1\\rangle\\right\\| \\\\  &=\\sqrt{\\left(5\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-1\\right)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{25+4+1} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\mathrm{d}S &=\\iint_D\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  &=\\iint_D \\sqrt{30}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559993 :question/problem "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559998 :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560005 :question/problem "![](images/13/137a452f1b378359fdd974b11781dddf1e7e9b469d864699b044eaf1cc1a7c20.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560007 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560017 :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560020 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560021 :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560028 :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{3 r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((6))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560038 :question/problem "![](images/81/819f039dfc5397bf393de37bbb17f4cb8888ce53a5370bfdf13d172697ffd2f7.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[(\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =\\left(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560045 :question/problem "![](images/44/44fada7635b47f3f7ef635b0466f21d87c71b52e7f39fddb5d36c16472f4ce82.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the second quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[r^{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((0))-((1))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((0)-(1)) \\\\ & =- 8 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $- 8 \\sqrt{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560069 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=5$, $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(5x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =5+0+0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}5\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}5\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[5x]_{x=0}^{x=5}\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =(25-0)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =300.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560071 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=2$, $x=3$, $y=1$, $y=2$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=2}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(27-8)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =114.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560077 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=2$, $x=4$, $y=1$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=2}^{x=4}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=4}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(64-8)\\cdot (16-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1680.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560078 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1$, $x=2$, $y=1$, $y=2$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560090 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=4$, $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(3x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}3\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[3x]_{x=0}^{x=4}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(12-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560100 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=2$, $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=5$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{5}2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[2x]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =(4-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560104 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1$, $x=3$, $y=1$, $y=3$, $z=2$, $z=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=3}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =(27-1)\\cdot (9-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =416.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560117 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=2$, $x=3$, $y=2$, $y=3$, $z=2$, $z=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=2}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=2}^{y=3}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =(27-8)\\cdot (9-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =190.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560121 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=4$, $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(7x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =7+0+0 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}7\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}7\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}7\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[7x]_{x=0}^{x=4}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(28-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =168.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560124 :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=2$, $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=5$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(8x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =8+0+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}8\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{5}8\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[8x]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =(16-0)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560130 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{4})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{2},\\,y=t^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 3 (t^{4})^{2})\\,i+(- 3 (t^{2})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 3 t^{8})\\,i+(- 3 t^{4})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{4})j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(4 t^{3})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 3 t^{8})\\,i+(- 3 t^{4})\\,j)\\cdot ((2 t)\\,i+(4 t^{3})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 t^{8})\\cdot (2 t)+(- 3 t^{4})\\cdot (4 t^{3}) \\\\  & =- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}(- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560131 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{3})\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{3},\\,y=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =((t^{2})^{2})\\,i+(- (t^{3})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(t^{4})\\,i+(- t^{6})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})j \\\\  & =(3 t^{2})\\,i+(2 t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t^{4})\\,i+(- t^{6})\\,j)\\cdot ((3 t^{2})\\,i+(2 t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t^{4})\\cdot (3 t^{2})+(- t^{6})\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560133 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{2},\\,y=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 (t^{3})^{2})\\,i+(- 4 (t^{2})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 t^{6})\\,i+(- 4 t^{4})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 2 t^{6})\\,i+(- 4 t^{4})\\,j)\\cdot ((2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 2 t^{6})\\cdot (2 t)+(- 4 t^{4})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{3}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}(- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560151 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- x - 4 y)\\,i+(x - 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- x - 4 y)\\,i+(x - 3 y)\\,j \\\\  & =(- (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) - 4 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+((t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) - 3 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =4 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =t^{2} \\left(4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560152 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 3 x + 3 y)\\,i+(- x - 4 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y=t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 3 x + 3 y)\\,i+(- x - 4 y)\\,j \\\\  & =(- 3 (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) + 3 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,i+(- (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}) - 4 (t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}))\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\  & =(- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j)\\cdot ((- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j) \\\\  & =(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =(- 3 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (- t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\  & =- 3 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\  & =t^{2} \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560171 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- x y^{2})\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,2]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- x y^{2})\\,i+(- 8 y)\\,j \\\\  & =(- (e^{t}) (\\sqrt{t})^{2})\\,i+(- 8 (\\sqrt{t}))\\,j \\\\  & =(- t e^{t})\\,i+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =(e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- t e^{t})\\,i+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\,j)\\cdot ((e^{t})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- t e^{t})\\cdot (e^{t})+(- 8 \\sqrt{t})\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =- t e^{2 t} - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{2}(- t e^{2 t} - 4)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
